\s13 \li1463 \fi-758 \ri1007 \ql \tx1461 \f0 \b \fs52 \f5 \b0 \fs73 p\tab \f0 \b \fs52 NSHelpManager\
\fs80\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s31 \li4231 \fi-2719 \ri1007 \ql \tx4233 \f0 \b \fs24 Inherits From:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 NSObject\
\fs20\
\pard \s21 \li4233 \fi-2721 \ri1007 \ql \tx4233 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Conforms To:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 NSObject (NSObject)\
\fs20\
\s22 \li4233 \fi-2721 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Declared In:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 AppKit/NSHelpManager.h\
\fs20\
\fs20\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSHelpManager provides a platform-independent approach to displaying on-line help. An application contains one instance of NSHelpManager. Your application\'27s code rarely needs to access NSHelpManager directly. Instead, you use Interface Builder and Project Builder to set up on-line help for your application.\
\fs14\
\fs28 OpenStep applications can run on multiple platforms, and each platform provides its own support for on-line help. It\'27s important to users that applications use the native on-line help system (on Microsoft Windows, for instance, users want the Microsoft Windows help system and don\'27t want to have to learn how to use a different help system), so NSHelpManager does not provide a comprehensive solution for presenting help. Instead, it provides cross-platform support for context-sensitive help and allows you to present more comprehensive help (conceptual and task-based help) in any way you choose.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Context Help\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Context-sensitive help (also referred to as context help) gives the user a small amount of information when they help-click an interface item. For example, if the user help-clicks a menu item called \'aaCopy,\'ba they should get context help that says something like \'aaCopies the currently selected text to the pasteboard.\'ba This text appears in a small window near where the user help-clicked, and the window disappears when the user clicks anywhere else in the application.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Help-clicking is performed in any one of several ways, depending on the platform and the hardware used. On Mach platforms, help-clicking is performed when the user holds down the Help key, the F1 key, or the Alternate and Control keys while pressing the mouse button. On Microsoft Windows platforms, users press Shift-F1 and then press the mouse button to display context-sensitive help. Some Microsoft Windows applications also have a What\'27s This menu item on the Help menu. When the user selects this item, the next mouse click displays context-sensitive help.\
\fs14\
\fs28 To provide context-sensitive help for your application, follow these steps:\
\fs14\
\fs2\
\fs28 \pard \s52 \li2340 \fi-376 \ri1007 \ql \tx2340 \tx3981 \tx4359 1.\tab For each interface item that needs context help, create an RTF or RTFD file containing the text and any images you want to appear when the user help-clicks that interface item. Try to keep the text as brief as possible and the images as small as possible. \
\fs16\
\pard \s4 \li2338 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2338 \tx3753 \tx4146 \fs28 The text that you write will appear in a small window just under the cursor when the user help-clicks an interface item. If the user help-clicks near the edge of the screen, text may appear off-screen. (This is especially prevalent when the user help-clicks a menu item on the Mach platform.) Use hard returns in your text so that the window will be as narrow as possible.\
\fs14\
\fs2\
\fs28 \pard \s53 \li2340 \fi-376 \ri1007 \ql \tx2340 \tx3981 \tx4359 2.\tab If you don\'27t need to localize your context help files, in Project Builder simply add these files to your project under Context Help.\
\fs16\
\pard \s4 \li2338 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2338 \tx3753 \tx4146 \fs28 If you do need to localize your context help files, first copy the files into the appropriate \b .lproj\b0  directory of your project, then add them to the project.\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 In Interface Builder, connect each interface item to its context help file by doing the following: \
\fs14\
\fs2\
\fs28 \pard \s52 \li2340 \fi-376 \ri1007 \ql \tx2340 \tx3981 \tx4359 1.\tab Bring up the Interface Builder inspector and choose the Help display. The Help display lists all the context help files associated with your application. (You may have to quit and restart Interface Builder to get this to occur.)\
\fs16\
\s53 \fs28 2.\tab Select an interface item.\
\fs16\
\fs28 3.\tab In the Inspector, choose the appropriate help file.\
\fs16\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 When you build your application, \b /usr/bin/compileHelp\b0  packages your help files into a property list named \b Help.plist\b0 . NSHelpManager knows how to extract context help from an \b Help.plist\b0  file.  \
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Comprehensive Help\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Most applications provide some form of on-line help that is more comprehensive and detailed than context-sensitive help, such as conceptual or task help. NSHelpManager allows you to provide this sort of comprehensive help in any way you choose. Some help authors prefer to provide comprehensive help in HTML using a World-Wide Web browser; others use tools such as Digital Librarian or Concurrence; on Microsoft Windows a full-featured native help system is available.\
\fs14\
\fs28 When the user chooses the Help menu item, NSHelpManager simply asks NSWorkspace to open the help file you have specified for your application. That file should be the starting point of your help, and should allow users to access whatever information they might need. \
\fs14\
\fs28 To specify a help file for your application, do one of the following:\
\fs14\
\pard \s5 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab In Project Builder, specify the name of the help file in the Project Attributes inspector. (If you are creating an application that will run on both the Mach and Windows platforms, you need to enter this file twice\'d0once for Mach and once for Windows). The specified value can be a full or relative path, and if it is relative, it is assumed to be a resource in the application wrapper. \
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab As an alternative, you can place the help file in your application wrapper and name it after your application. If you haven\'27t specified a help file, NSHelpManager looks in the application wrapper for an appropriately named file.\
\fs14\
\pard \s4 \li2338 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2338 \tx3753 \tx4146 \fs28 On Mach, it must be an RTF file called \i appName\b \i0 .rtf\b0  (where \i appName\i0  is the name of the application).\
\fs14\
\fs28 On Microsoft Windows, it must be a Windows help file called \i appName\b \i0 .hlp\b0 .\
\fs14\
\pard \s46 \li2310 \fi-798 \ri1007 \ql \tx2308 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Note:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 It\'27s common for Windows applications to have more than one command under the Help menu and to have each command open a different help file. To implement this, connect each of the Help menu commands to a different action method. The actions methods should send \b openFile:\b0  to the shared NSWorkspace object to open the appropriate help file. For example:\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 \tab \
\tab [[NSWorkspace sharedWorkspace] openFile:@"AppKit.hlp"];\
\fs70\
